    "advanced": {
      "title": "Venir de + Infinitive — Recent Past, Conjugation & Time Expressions",
      "explanation": "Venir de + infinitive expresses an action that just happened, typically within the last few minutes or hours. Conjugate venir (je viens, tu viens, il/elle/on vient, nous venons, vous venez, ils/elles viennent), then add 'de' (or 'd'' before a vowel) followed by the infinitive. Examples: Je viens de manger (I just ate), Elle vient de partir (She just left), Nous venons d'arriver (We just arrived). Common time markers: à l'instant, tout à l'heure, il y a quelques minutes. Contrast with passé composé: venir de focuses on immediate past; passé composé is more general past. Advanced note: You can also say 'venir de' in past tense (venais de, venait de) for an action that had just happened in the past: 'Elle venait de partir quand j'ai appelé' (She had just left when I called).",
      "examples": [
        {
          "french": "Je viens de composer le numéro.",
          "english": "I just dialed the number."
        },
        {
          "french": "Tu viens de raccrocher?",
          "english": "Did you just hang up?"
        },
        {
          "french": "Il vient de répondre au téléphone.",
          "english": "He just answered the phone."
        },
        {
          "french": "Nous venons de rappeler notre ami.",
          "english": "We just called our friend back."
        },
        {
          "french": "Vous venez d'arriver?",
          "english": "Did you just arrive?"
        },
        {
          "french": "Ils viennent de terminer l'appel.",
          "english": "They just finished the call."
        }
      ],
